Profile-guided build of n72-ui
==============================

n72ui.profdata is an execution profile of n72-ui (clang IR-level
instrumentation, -fprofile-generate) taken on the iPod touch 2G with a
wallpaper set, panel lit, through the fake-touch harness: home page swipes
(slow and flung), Files (/usr/lib, /root) and Logs scrolls, the Sound,
Display and Wi-Fi pages, typing on the keyboard, and --bench. build.sh
uses it whenever it is here (NOPGO=1: not), with hot/cold splitting at the
LTO link.

Measured 2026-11-14 on the device (same scripted touches twice, panel lit):
render time per frame while scrolling ~5% lower (Logs 7.8 -> 7.4 ms, Files
8.7-9.1 -> 7.8-7.9 ms without the 25-30 ms spikes); text drawn into a canvas
6.85 -> 6.19 ms; frame rates unchanged (the scrolls wait for the display,
55-64 fps either way); --bench and text checksums identical. -O3 with the
profile: canvas text 5.79 ms, the rest no better and a 45 KB larger
binary, so -O2 stays.

A function changed since the profile is built as without one (clang checks
a hash per function); make a new one after larger changes:

  1. N72_CC=clang PGO_GEN=1 sh n72-ui/build.sh
     (n72-ui-build-clang-pgogen/; the runtime is n72-gles/pgo-rt/libclang_rt.profile-armhf.a)
  2. On the device: that binary as /root/n72-ui-pgogen, the training script
     (pgo/train.sh: harness runs with
     LLVM_PROFILE_FILE=/root/pgo/ui-%p.profraw; the counters are written
     when n72-ui is stopped with SIGTERM, or at the end of --bench).
  3. llvm-profdata merge -o pgo/n72ui.profdata raw/*.profraw
  4. Build as usual.

Static functions are named in the profile by their file's base name
(build.sh: -static-func-full-module-prefix=false), not by the full path
clang uses by default: the profile was taken from a build under /work, and
the builds on the Mac, under another path, silently found nothing for the
884 static functions in it (60 % of its counts, the blend loops and the
style lookups among them). 2026-11-23: the names in n72ui.profdata were
rewritten to base names (llvm-profdata merge --text, the directory part of
each "path;function" name removed, merged back; the counts are untouched).
To see what a build finds: compile a file with -Wbackend-plugin -mllvm
-pgo-warn-missing-function=true in place of build.sh's -Wno- flags.

gcc (the compiler the rule asks for; no gcc profile exists yet): the same
steps with the default compiler. PGO_GEN=1 sh n72-ui/build.sh gives
n72-ui-build-pgogen/; on the device it writes /tmp/n72ui-pgo/obj#NAME.gcda
at exit (PGO_DIR); those files go in pgo/gcc/. The names are relative to
the output folder (build.sh), so the profiled build finds them from any
folder: checked 2026-11-23, the 131 names in the instrumented binary are
the 131 a profiled -O3 build in another folder looks up. A missing file
or a function changed since the profile shows as a warning in the build.
